# grout daemon arguments. `-t` = test-mode (no hugepages), suitable for the
# lab/MVP path and for running inside the CRA netns without host DPDK tuning.
# For a production fast path clear `-t` after preparing hugepages + vfio-pci.
# `-m 666` makes /run/grout.sock world-rw so FRR's dplane_grout (unprivileged
# `frr` user) can drive grout's EVPN fdb/FIB; without it zebra logs
# "Permission denied" and never syncs grout's interfaces/routes.
#
# `--max-mtu 9216` raises grout's interface MTU ceiling, which defaults to 1800.
# The fabric runs at 9100 like the FRR flavour, and grout refuses any port above
# the ceiling with ERANGE ("Numerical result out of range") at creation time --
# and the port MTU can only be set while grout still owns the netdev, since once
# the tap is moved to the host `interface set port ... mtu` fails with EPERM.
# The ceiling is not free: the mbuf size is
# `rte_align32pow2(headroom + eth + vlan + max_mtu)`, so 9216 rounds every mbuf
# up to 16 KiB and the fixed 64 Ki-entry packet pool to ~1 GiB, against ~128 MiB
# at the default. That is what the heap below has to absorb; anything between
# 9100 and 16384 costs exactly the same, so there is nothing to gain by shaving
# it closer to 9100.
#
# Everything after `--` is passed straight through to DPDK's EAL. The trailing
# `-m 6144` pins the EAL heap size, which must be sized together with the CRA
# container's memory limit (see CRA_MEMORY in cra-start.sh, 12288m). In test mode
# the heap is a reservation rather than an upfront allocation -- a fully
# configured node measures ~2.1 GiB resident -- but every allocation is served
# from it, so it has to cover the worst case, not the average.
#
# Both directions of getting this wrong fail quietly:
#
#   Too large relative to the container limit, and EAL's heap alone claims the
#   whole cgroup. grout is OOM-killed partway through startup and restarts
#   clean -- without the ports node setup had already created -- so nothing
#   reports a failure and the only symptom appears minutes later as BGP stuck
#   in Idle. grout's own default is exactly 2048, so leaving it implicit under
#   a 2048m limit put it precisely on that edge, which is also why it looked
#   intermittent: identical nodes came up or died depending on reclaim.
#
#   Too small, and grout starts but cannot allocate the per-VRF FIBs. It still
#   reports ready and `interface show` works, so the node looks healthy, but
#   every address or route operation fails with ENONET ("Machine is not on the
#   network") and no traffic is ever forwarded. 1024 is already too small.
#
# The heap has to cover the ports and the per-VRF FIBs at the same time. Each
# port brings its own 16 KiB-mbuf pools (see --max-mtu above) and each VRF
# allocates both FIBs up front: an IPv4 DIR24_8 FIB alone reserves a 2^24-entry
# table. At grout's default FIB sizes two extra VRFs exhausted a 2048 MB heap;
# with the trunk plus two uplink ports, even the reduced sizes the operator uses
# for tenant VRFs (see DefaultVRFRib4Routes in pkg/cra-grout) did not fit in
# 4096 MB. 5120 was the smallest that did, and 6144 keeps real headroom. The
# container limit needs headroom of its own on top: the per-port pools and
# queues live outside the heap and grow with the number of workload ports, and a
# node that reaches its cgroup limit does not get a clean OOM kill -- grout died
# with SIGSEGV in the middle of creating a port.
# (Note grout's own `-m` is the API socket mode; EAL's `-m` is memory.)
#
# `-a` (--adaptive-irq) makes an idle datapath worker arm its rxq interrupts and
# block instead of polling. Without it every worker spins a CPU at 100% for as
# long as grout runs, whether or not a packet ever arrives, and the lab runs
# three of these on one machine. On a four-CPU CI runner that is the whole
# machine, and the damage lands on the *cluster*, not on the datapath: liveness
# probes to localhost time out, etcd answers "request timed out", multus gives
# up on the API server after a minute and bring-up takes an hour. Restricting
# the CRA's cpuset (see CRA_CPUS in cra-start.sh) bounds how many CPUs can be
# taken, but only this stops them from being burnt while nothing is happening --
# which is exactly the phase where the cluster needs them.
#
# The flag is safe to pass unconditionally: a PMD whose rxqs cannot raise
# interrupts makes grout log "disabling adaptive-irq" for that worker and fall
# back to polling, so the worst case is the behaviour we had before. The `tap`
# PMD, which is what every port in this lab is, does support them.
ARGS="-t -a -m 666 --max-mtu 9216 -- -m 6144"
